  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 232 AM CST Sat Nov 28 2026\n\nA weak shortwave is pulsing across the region tonight, bringing some \nlight rain, perhaps a few snowflakes. However, this wave is moving \ninto an area of much drier air. Surface observations are not \nindicating any of the returns on radar are reaching the ground.\nThe chances for any of the precipitation reaching the ground \ndissipates by just after sunrise. \n\nThe next substantial rain comes tonight into Sunday as a shortwave \ndigs into the Plains from the northern Rockies, developing into a \nlow pressure system over Oklahoma, with the low tracking across \nsouthern IL on Sunday. With temperatures in the upper 30s tonight \nand mid 40s to low 50s Sunday, rain is the expected precipitation \ntype. PWATs look to be around 0.8-1.1 inches. This widespread rain \nevent has a 30-60% chance of exceeding 0.75 inches of rain east of I-\n55, based off LREF 24 hour precipitation probabilities. The NBM \nstatistics show higher QPF amounts, with the mean QPF being\n0.8-1.4 inches. \n\nA high pressure center builds over the central part of the United \nStates following the low's departure. This will generate a gradual \nwarming trend into the new week. By Thursday and Friday, highs will \nbe in the low 60s, with a 40-60% chance of highs exceeding 60 \ndegrees from the NBM. The LREF shows lower probs in comparison. \nIncreased moisture return is depicted by the synoptic models late in \nthe week. The GFS and Euro are showing a late week system moving \nthrough with thunderstorms possible. There is still little under a \nweek until this shall come to pass, so it is expected that the low \nlocation and track will continue to shift. 60+ temperatures in \nDecember could be a bad omen for unsettled or even severe weather. \nAs of now, the models aren't showing any significant amount of CAPE \n(couple hundred Joules), but bulk wind shear of 50-60+ knots. As \nthis is still a week out, future forecasts will monitor trends \nas things evolve.",
